Correlation DESeq2 vs TMM scaling: 0.9999 
 The two methods agree closely on well-behaved data. They differ when a few
 genes take a big share of reads, or when composition shifts globally.

======================================================================
  3. Composition bias: a simulation you should never forget
======================================================================
converting counts to integer mode
Median log2FC of the 95% UNCHANGED genes:
   CPM (total-count) normalization : -0.514 
   median-of-ratios               : -0.006 
 CPM makes every unchanged gene look DOWN-regulated, because the up-regulated
 genes 'stole' sequencing depth. Median-of-ratios (and TMM) assume most genes
 are not DE and correct for it.
  saved: results/03_norm/composition_bias_sim.png

======================================================================
  4. Gene length: RPKM/FPKM and TPM
======================================================================
Column sums -- TPM always sums to 1e6, RPKM does not:
     SRR1039508 SRR1039509 SRR1039512 SRR1039513 SRR1039516 SRR1039517
TPM     1000000    1000000    1000000    1000000    1000000    1000000
RPKM     281777     284202     273369     271390     286533     269871
     SRR1039520 SRR1039521
TPM     1000000    1000000
RPKM     266696     256766

TPM is good for comparing genes WITHIN a sample (gene A vs gene B).
 For DE BETWEEN samples, gene length cancels out (same gene, same length), so
 DE tools take RAW counts + size factors. Never give TPM/FPKM to DESeq2/edgeR:
 they need the counts to model the count noise.
Spearman cor(length, raw count) = 0.424   cor(length, TPM) = 0.016 

======================================================================
  5. Which normalized values for what?
======================================================================
  DE testing ...................... raw counts (tool normalizes internally)
  PCA / clustering / heatmaps ..... VST or rlog (DESeq2), logCPM w/ prior (edgeR)
  Plotting one gene ............... normalized counts, log scale
  Comparing genes in one sample ... TPM
  Sharing with collaborators ...... raw counts + metadata (+ TPM for browsing)
Wrote normalized counts and TPM tables to results/03_norm 
